  "seoBody": "<div class=\"key-takeaways\"><strong>Key takeaways</strong><ul><li>Street works in England and Wales sit under the New Roads and Street Works Act 1991 (NRSWA), with reinstatements judged against the Specification for the Reinstatement of Openings in Highways (SROH)</li><li>Reinstatements are inspected by category, and defects can trigger remedial notices and re-inspection charges from the highway authority</li><li>Most operatives never fill in a dedicated app on site; they complete it later from memory, and the record suffers</li><li>Quickler charges 20 pounds per active user per month, and dormant users are free, so a firm with a mix of gangs, subcontractors and office staff only pays for who actually works</li><li>The software captures evidence and generates the report; it does not lay the reinstatement or replace a qualified NRSWA operative or supervisor</li></ul></div><h2>What a street works inspection app is actually for</h2><p>Telecoms and utility contractors open the highway constantly, and every opening has to be made good and recorded. The record has its own audience: the highway authority that inspects the reinstatement, the client network owner, and the insurer or adjudicator if a reinstatement later fails. What they all want is an accurate, timestamped, photographed account of what was reinstated and how, and it has to survive scrutiny months later.</p><p>Software does not make a reinstatement compliant. A properly laid reinstatement to the SROH does. What software does is make the inspection record easier to complete correctly on site, harder to forget a category or a coring result, and faster to deliver. A good tool means the operative does not write up a reinstatement from memory once the barriers are down and the crew has moved on.</p><h2>NRSWA, the SROH and permit schemes</h2><p>In England and Wales, works in the highway are governed by the New Roads and Street Works Act 1991 (NRSWA). Reinstatements are judged against the Specification for the Reinstatement of Openings in Highways (SROH), the code of practice that sets materials, layer depths, compaction and the categories a reinstatement is inspected under. Many highway authorities run permit schemes, so an opening carries a permit reference and agreed conditions before work starts. Scotland operates an equivalent regime under its own roadworks legislation and Register.</p><p>The inspection record must name the reinstatement category, show the layers and materials, and photograph the finished make-good. Quickler captures each of these at the point of inspection rather than afterwards. It does not lay the reinstatement or judge whether it meets the SROH; the qualified operative and supervisor do that. Check the current specification and permit scheme that applies to your works.</p><h2>NRSWA qualifications and sign-off</h2><p>NRSWA sets competence requirements for the people carrying out and supervising street works. Operatives and supervisors hold registration cards for the units relevant to their role, from location of apparatus through excavation to reinstatement and compaction. The inspection record should tie the works to the qualified person who did them.</p><p>Quickler records the operative and supervisor against each inspection, with a timestamp, so the evidence shows who was competent. It does not verify a card's validity or replace the supervisor's judgement. Keep your own record of current registrations. This is general guidance, not legal advice; check the current NRSWA requirements for your works.</p><h2>Categories, coring and remedial notices</h2><p>A reinstatement is inspected against the category the opening falls into, which sets the standard the surface, binder and sub-base must meet. Highway authorities take cores to check thickness and compaction, and a failed reinstatement can attract a remedial notice and re-inspection charges. Recording your own coring or sampling results, the category, and photographs of the make-good at the point of inspection gives you the evidence to defend the works if a defect is raised later.</p><h2>What to look for in a street works inspection app</h2><ul><li><strong>On-site completion:</strong> the tool should make it easier to complete the record at the excavation, not afterwards</li><li><strong>Photo evidence at the point of inspection:</strong> each reinstatement tied to a timestamped image, not a folder sorted out that evening</li><li><strong>Category and permit capture:</strong> the record should carry the reinstatement category and permit reference the authority expects</li><li><strong>Only pay for who works:</strong> charging for every seat punishes firms for their gangs, subcontractors and office staff. Quickler charges 20 pounds per active user per month, dormant users free, unlimited users</li><li><strong>Evidence trail:</strong> timestamp, operative, supervisor, location, every observation, for the remedial notice or dispute that surfaces months later</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Honest note:</strong> Quickler captures the evidence and generates the report. It does not lay the reinstatement, judge SROH compliance, or make an inspection valid. It records what a qualified NRSWA operative and supervisor did, as evidence. The competent, registered person carries the compliance. This is general guidance, not legal advice. <a href=\"/pages/help/index.html#contact-form-section\">Ask us</a> if that distinction matters for your firm.</div><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What is a street works inspection app?</h3><p>It is any tool that helps an operative record a highway reinstatement inspection and produce a report: the reinstatement category, the layers, coring or sampling results, permit reference and photos of the make-good. The options range from a clipboard, to generic audit apps like iAuditor, to conversation-based tools like quickler that run the workflow over WhatsApp so there is no app to install.</p><h3>Does a reinstatement inspection have to follow a specific format?</h3><p>Street works in England and Wales sit under the New Roads and Street Works Act 1991, and reinstatements are judged against the Specification for the Reinstatement of Openings in Highways (SROH). There is no single mandated app format, but the record must capture the reinstatement category, materials and layers, and the evidence the highway authority expects. Scotland has an equivalent regime. Check the current specification and any permit scheme that applies to your works.</p><h3>Can I run street works inspections over WhatsApp?</h3><p>Yes. Quickler's workflows run over the WhatsApp Business API. The operative receives each question in their existing WhatsApp chat, replies with text, a voice note or a photo, and the completed report generates automatically. No separate app or login is required, and quickler manages the WhatsApp Business API account on the firm's behalf.</p><h3>Does the app make my reinstatement compliant?</h3><p>No. Quickler records the inspection and stores the evidence with a timestamp and photos. It does not lay the reinstatement, judge whether it meets the SROH, or replace a qualified NRSWA operative and supervisor. The competent, registered person carries the compliance; the app carries the record. This is general guidance, not legal advice.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/telecoms-inspection-software-uk.html\">Telecoms field inspection software</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/antenna-rf-safety-audit-uk.html\">Antenna and RF safety audits</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/mast-tower-inspection-checklist-uk.html\">Mast and tower inspection checklist</a></li></ul>",
